<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wiener Neustadt</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wiener Neustadt is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, founded in 1194 by Duke Leopold V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> as a fortified frontier town against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Emperor Frederick III often resided in its castle in the 15th century, and he and his son Maximilian I are associated with it. The castle has housed the Theresian Military Academy since 1751.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wiener-neustadt.jpg" alt="Wiener Neustadt" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C.Stadler/Bwag · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wiener_Neustadt_-_Hauptplatz_(2).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area once belonged to the County of Pitten, inherited by Margrave Ottokar III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> in 1158. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otakars">Otakars</a> died out, the Duchy of Styria passed to the Austrian House of Babenberg under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgenberg-pact">Georgenberg Pact</a>. Duke Leopold V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> established the town called Neustadt in 1194 and paid for a fortress close to the Hungarian border with the ransom for the English king Richard the Lionheart, whom he had captured and held at Dürnstein Castle.</p><p>In 1241 a small Mongol squadron raided the town and was repulsed by Duke Frederick and his knights, and in 1246 it was the scene of a Hungarian victory over the Austrians. Its privileges let it prosper, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-marchfeld">Battle on the Marchfeld</a> in 1278 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a>.</p><p>In the fifteenth century the town grew when Emperor Frederick III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> took up residence there, and he founded the Diocese of Wiener Neustadt in 1469. His wife, Eleanor of Portugal, died there in 1467. His son Maximilian I held his court in the town and is buried at St George's Cathedral.</p><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> conquered the city in August 1487 after a two-year siege, and Maximilian reconquered his native city in 1490. In the sixteenth century it lost its status as an imperial residence. In 1609 Emperor Rudolf II granted the Bohemian Protestants the Majestätsbrief, a patent of equal rights, at Neustadt, and its revocation helped to precipitate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Wiener Neustadt lies some 50 kilometres (31 mi) south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>. Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> established the Theresian Military Academy in the imperial castle to train officers for the Austrian army. An earthquake destroyed the city in 1768 and a fire in 1834 destroyed it again, after which it was almost entirely rebuilt and became an industrial town, especially after the Austrian Southern Railway opened in 1841.</p><p>In 1909 the first official Austrian airfield was inaugurated north of the city. In January 1918 a strike began there among workers of the Austro-Daimler factory, who opposed the war and the halving of the flour ration. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, repeated bombing left only 18 of 4,000 buildings undamaged. The castle, rebuilt to its original appearance, still houses the academy, and the late Romanesque cathedral was consecrated in 1279.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wiener Neustadt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Theresian Military Academy was founded in the castle of <b>Wiener Neustadt</b> in Lower Austria, once a residence of Emperor Frederick III.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-14">14 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>These duchies were ruled as Inner Austria by Leopold's descendants in the 15th century; Frederick III, who resided at Graz and <b>Wiener Neustadt</b>, later reunited the dynasty's lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/inner-austria">Inner Austria</a></li></ul></section>
